ID: Ch1-Intro1
TEXT:

There is an old saying about the fine line between courage and stupidity. Standing on scorched loam under the twisted, violet-veined canopy of a Varathian forest with the horizon detonating in silent blooms of arcane fire, I have almost certainly crossed it.

My name is Barry. I am twenty-eight years old, and on paper, entirely unremarkable. No innate gift, no formal academy pedigree, no noble bloodline. Just a satchel of theoretical notes, a notched dagger at my belt, and an obsession that took root when I was five and never let go: I want to become a mage. I spent twenty-three years scouring guild libraries, hunting down obscure treatises, and chasing every rumor across the continents to find a loophole in the natural order.

Eventually, I found one.

The condition is simple enough on parchment: win an open tournament against the deadliest mages alive. Madness, by any sane standard. But the teleportation circle collapsed into ozone behind me ten minutes ago, and there is no return ticket. The only road left runs straight through.

Looking out at the smoke rising above the treeline, feeling the damp pine needles give way beneath my boots and knowing my ordinary life is finished, what I feel most of all is...

choice("Excited", Ch1-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro2, v_ch1_intro_feeling = 1)
choice("Calm", Ch1-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro2, v_ch1_intro_feeling = 2)
choice("Afraid", Ch1-Intro2, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro2, v_ch1_intro_feeling = 3)


ID: Ch1-Intro2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch1_intro_feeling == 1) {
...an electric, restless hum in my chest. Maybe I have completely lost my mind. By all rights, terror ought to have frozen my knees. Instead, adrenaline prickles beneath my skin like static. Twenty-three years of theoretical prep, and I am finally standing on the same ground as the greatest casters in the world. The lethal risks ahead are real, but they pale next to the prize waiting at the end: win this tournament, and I finally become a mage.
}
#if(v_ch1_intro_feeling == 2) {
...a steady, icy focus. Years of running worst-case calculations have stripped away the shock. The odds are absurd, the participants are lethal, and one slip means an unmarked grave in Varathian soil. But I came here with my eyes open. I have the knowledge, I have the preparation, and I did not cross oceans just to fold at the starting line. I am going to win this tournament and take what is mine.
}
#if(v_ch1_intro_feeling == 3) {
...cold dread coiled in my gut. Reading about lethal elemental duels by candlelight is one thing; standing in the blast zone is another entirely. Every theoretical plan I spent weeks refining evaporates in the face of raw mortality. The mages on this island could incinerate me before I draw breath. Yet backing out is not an option—I had twenty-three years to quit, and I chose this path. Terror or not, I have to see it through to the end.
}
A concussive crack rips through the trees three hundred yards out, rattling the branches and scattering birds in a panicked screech. The opening clashes are already underway, far too close for comfort. Engaging any caster in a direct contest right now is suicide. Survival means keeping low and letting the heavy hitters bleed each other first.

I cut south, away from the smoke, staying in the thick brush along the forest edge. Muffled voices drift from a small clearing ahead. Slipping behind the trunk of a massive, moss-caked oak, I ease forward just enough to peer around the bark.

The skirmish is already over. The victor stands with arms crossed, looking like an ordinary back-alley cutpurse—grease-stained tunic, scuffed leather trousers, and a bandolier heavy with balanced throwing daggers. A greasy smirk cuts across his unkempt dark beard. A few paces away, his opponent leans heavily on a notched broadsword, breathing in ragged gasps. A heavy kite shield lies half-buried in the dirt, and fresh blood seeps from the joints of his plate armor.

The defeated warrior is a towering, broad-shouldered man in his mid-forties, dark-skinned and bald, with a stark X-shaped scar gouged into his forehead. The descriptions in the eastern chronicles match him to a letter: Daren, the legendary healer in armor. Yet here he is, brought to his knees by a common brigand. 

The man in the tunic twirls a dagger between his fingers, voice dripping with mockery.

"The legendary Daren! The savior of the Eastern Continent. The healer in armor. They say you crossed the world putting down horrors for helpless villagers—that you once shattered ten rock golems single-handed just to pull a child from the rubble. Turns out fairy tales are cheap, aye? I knew all that tavern rot about giant-slayers was pure codswallop. If the rest of the big names in this tournament are as soft as you, I'll have the crown by nightfall!"

He chuckles, a grating, wheezing rasp—then snaps his head toward my tree.

"Wait. Who's there?"

I press my back flat against the rough wood, killing my breath in my throat.

"I know you're behind that oak, coward. Step out, or one of my daggers will come fetch you!"

choice("Stay silent", Ch1-Cutthroat Dave, v_current_scene = Ch1-Cutthroat Dave, v_ch1_show_yourself = 1)
choice("Show myself", Ch1-Cutthroat Dave, v_ac_ch1_coward = 1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Cutthroat Dave, v_ch1_show_yourself = 2)
choice(""I see no reason to show myself to you."", Ch1-Cutthroat Dave, v_current_scene = Ch1-Cutthroat Dave, v_ch1_show_yourself = 3)


ID: Ch1-Cutthroat Dave
TEXT:

achievement("Who are you calling a coward?",v_ac_ch1_coward)
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 1) {
I hold my breath and don't make a sound. He has no line of sight; it could be a blind probe. If I stay still, he will write it off as forest noise.

"Have it your way," the rogue calls out. A whip-like crack splits the air. A throwing dagger streaks past the trunk, banks impossible ninety degrees in mid-flight, and drives deep into my left shoulder, pinning sleeve and muscle to the oak. A raw gasp tears from my throat as I grab at the hilt.

"You won't wrench that loose. My steel answers only to me. Now that I have your mark, I'm going to—"

He cuts off abruptly.

"A stillwater? Playing the idiot to make me drop my guard? Lulling me into a false sense of security? Think you're clever, do you? Well, it won't work on me. I'm too sharp for your games!"
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 2) {
I draw a slow breath, steadying my pulse as best I can, and step out from behind the trunk into the open clearing, fixing my eyes on the knife-thrower.

"Who are you calling a coward?" I ask him.

The rogue freezes, taking my measure from boot to collar.

"A stillwater, eh?" he says. He aims for swagger, but his voice catches on a thin, nervous rasp.
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 3) {
"I see no reason to show myself to you," I tell him.

The rogue locks onto the direction of my voice, fingers twitching near his bandolier—then he goes completely rigid.

"A stillwater, eh?" he says. He aims for swagger, but his voice catches on a thin, nervous rasp.
}
The sudden shift in tone makes tactical sense. The existence of stillwaters is the entire reason I gambled on entering this tournament in the first place.

Stillwaters are freakish anomalies in the magical order, known for catastrophic, god-scale destruction. Twenty years ago, one of them leveled the metropolis of Olmnar in the span of a single heartbeat, with nothing more than a casual flick of his fingers. One of the greatest cities on Varathia was reduced to glass and ash in seconds.

The crucial detail is their magical footprint. Normally, every living creature radiates ambient energy. Even a non-caster can feel the distinct pressure of a mage's aura in the air, sizing up their reserve before steel clashes. 

Stillwaters, however, project the exact same flat, faint aura as an ordinary human. And when a tournament draws the deadliest spellcasters in the world to one island, running into someone who registers as a mundane civilian leaves only two logical conclusions: either an ordinary mortal was suicidal enough to sign his own death warrant, or he is standing in front of a stillwater capable of erasing him with a word.

"You don't scare me, stillwater!" the rogue snarls, trying to shout down his own tremor. "I'll put enough steel through you to drain you dry—you'll beg for mercy before the sun goes down! I bet your kind's reputation is just as bloated as this broken relic on the ground."

choice("Give him until the count of ten to retreat", Ch1-Retreat, v_ac_ch1_die = 1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Retreat)
choice("Imply that I'm the one who destroyed the city of Olmnar", Ch1-Imply, v_current_scene = Ch1-Imply)


ID: Ch1-Retreat
TEXT:

achievement("Off to a good start",v_ac_ch1_die)
"I'm going to give you until the count of ten to get out of my sight," I tell him.

"Hah! Empty bluster. Arm yourself, fool! Today, I make my name!"

#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 1) {
Leather snaps as the rogue flings a volley blind into the foliage. I wrench desperately at the hilt embedded in my shoulder, but the enchanted steel bites deeper into the wood. Five whistling daggers shoot past the trunk, pivot in midair with sickening precision, and dive back inward. I twist, but the pin holds fast. Cold steel punches through chest and throat, the forest tilts, and the world goes black.
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 2) {
The rogue snaps his wrists, launching five daggers in a lethal spread. I throw myself sideways, but enchanted steel tracks faster than human reflexes. The blades tear through leather, muscle, and bone in a single concussive volley. The ground rushes up to meet me, and darkness takes the light.
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 3) {
The rogue flings a handful of daggers toward my voice. The blades whip past the brush, bank sharply in midair, and plunge through the undergrowth from behind. Steel tears into flesh before I can even drop. Pain flashes white-hot, my knees buckle into the loam, and the world dissolves into dark.
}

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: Ch1-Imply
TEXT:

"That's funny," I tell him. "That's almost the same thing the people of Olmnar told me. Before I burned their city to the ground."

#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 2) {
The rogue's hand freezes an inch above his bandolier. His gaze locks onto mine, pupils dilated, refusing to blink.
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself != 2) {
A suffocating quiet settles over the clearing. Neither of us moves.
}
"You're bluffing," the rogue says.

"Am I?" I let my eyes drift—slowly, deliberately—to the three daggers still embedded in the oak behind Daren. The blades are humming, trembling in their sheaths of bark. A faint sheen of sweat glistens on Dave's right forearm—the arm that controls them. The enchantment is tethered to his body. He cannot throw AND recall simultaneously. If his recalled blades are here, his bandolier is empty.

"You've committed all six blades," I say. "Three in the tree. Three in the healer. That leaves you with nothing but the knife in your boot and whatever prayers you know. So I'll ask one time. Walk away, or find out how I earned my reputation."

Three seconds stretch into an eternity. His eyes flick to the oak. To Daren. To his own empty bandolier. The calculation writes itself across his face: I should not know that. No one reads enchanted steel mechanics at a glance unless they've faced worse.

Finally, he lowers his chin, teeth grinding.

"You're in luck, stillwater. I'm feeling merciful today. I'll stay out of your path—just as soon as I finish this one here."

"You do that and you'll regret it," I say before my brain can stop my mouth.

The words hang in the air like a slap. The rogue recoils, staring in disbelief.

"What, are you joking? You're throwing down for this scrub?"

"If I have to. Do I have to?"

choice("Continue", Ch1-Imply2, v_current_scene = Ch1-Imply2)


ID: Ch1-Imply2
TEXT:

#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 1) {
The rogue holds still, jaw working as he weighs the gamble. With a low snarl, he sheathes his drawn daggers back into his bandolier and spits into the loam.

"I'll remember this, stillwater. Don't think I won't. You'll rue the day you crossed Cutthroat Dave. And the same goes for you, healer in armor. I'll be seeing both of you again soon."

He turns on his heel and strides into the brush on the far side of the clearing. The blade in my shoulder wrenchingly pulls itself free from flesh and bark, zipping through the leaves to slide into its master's sheath.
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 2) {
The rogue's face twists in a foul scowl, but he refuses the bait. Slowly, deliberately, he slots his daggers back into his bandolier and spits in the dirt near Daren's boots.

"I'll remember this, stillwater. Don't think I won't. I never forget a face. You'll rue the day you crossed Cutthroat Dave. And the same goes for you, healer in armor. I'll be seeing both of you again soon."

He spits again, turns on his heel, and vanishes into the undergrowth on the opposite side of the clearing.
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 3) {
The rogue holds still, jaw working as he weighs the gamble. With a low snarl, he sheathes his drawn daggers back into his bandolier and spits into the loam.

"I'll remember this, stillwater. Don't think I won't. You'll rue the day you crossed Cutthroat Dave. And the same goes for you, healer in armor. I'll be seeing both of you again soon."

He turns on his heel and stalks into the trees on the far side of the clearing.
}
Once the footsteps fade into the brush, the breath leaves my chest in a shaky hiss. My hands are trembling, and cold sweat clings to the back of my neck.

Six daggers. I counted six. I guessed that enchanted steel had a tether constraint based on a passage in Velran's Treatise on Animated Weapons—a book I read four years ago, in a library two continents away, because it mentioned a footnote about mana conductivity in throwing alloys. Four years of useless trivia, and it just saved a man's life. My life too, probably. The sheer absurdity of it buckles my knees.

Across the clearing, the armored knight slides his battered broadsword into its scabbard, scoops his shield from the dirt, and limps over toward me. His dark eyes are sharp—not grateful, not yet. Calculating.

"That was not a bluff," he says quietly. Not a question.

"What?"

"You read his weapons. You knew he was empty." He stops three paces away, studying me the way a surgeon studies a wound. "A stillwater would have simply killed him. You analyzed his enchantment and exploited a gap. Who does that?"

I have no answer that doesn't sound insane.

"Listen," he says, squaring his broad shoulders despite the blood on his mail. "These woods are crawling with killers far worse than that cutpurse, and you clearly don't have the power to back up what you just did a second time. I'll walk with you until we reach safer ground—a settlement, a checkpoint. After that, we reassess. Not because I owe you. Because anyone who thinks the way you just thought is more useful alive than dead. Fair enough?"

The offer catches me off-guard. No gratitude, no chivalric oath—just cold tactical assessment wrapped in a practical proposition.

"It's, uh... Barry. My name is Barry."

"Wait. Your name is Barry?"

A snort breaks through his stern expression, followed by an unrestrained bark of laughter. The solemn knightly cadence collapses entirely.

"That is the single strangest name for a mage I have ever encountered in all my travels. Especially for a stillwater. Is there something you care to share with me, Barry?"

choice("Tell him I'm actually an ordinary human", Ch1-Ordinary, v_current_scene = Ch1-Ordinary, v_ch1_stillwater = 2)
choice("Tell him my parents had a cruel sense of humor", Ch1-Humor, v_current_scene = Ch1-Humor, v_ch1_stillwater = 1)


ID: Ch1-Humor
TEXT:

"Yes. My parents had a cruel sense of humor. Speaking of names, aren't you supposed to be a hero of legend? How exactly does one as strong as you get bested so easily by someone named Cutthroat Dave?"

"Ah. Well. That..."

He rubs the back of his neck, sheepish.

"You see, Barry, raw power does not make a man invincible. Everyone has vulnerabilities. Mine happens to be that I never quite mastered the fundamentals of the standard shield spell."

"The shield spell? The basic kinetic ward every novice learns in their first semester? How does a renowned mage fumble that?"

"I did not say I cannot cast wards at all. Magical attacks, elemental bursts—I can deflect those without trouble. It is physical steel that slips through my barriers. That is why I wear plate. That is why I carry steel and oak. What began as a compromise became my entire combat doctrine. I apprenticed under an enchanting master to temper weapons, wove spells into my blade, and relied on white magic to mend whatever cuts found a seam. Watch."

#if(v_ch1_show_yourself == 1) {
He raises open palms. A brilliant, soothing white radiance blooms from his skin, washing the clearing in warm light. Where the light touches his bruised armor seams, the bleeding stanches and torn flesh stitches itself whole in seconds. He then steps over and extends a glowing hand toward my punctured left shoulder; the throbbing ache vanishes instantly, leaving clean, unblemished skin beneath my torn sleeve.
}
#if(v_ch1_show_yourself != 1) {
He raises open palms. A brilliant, soothing white radiance blooms from his skin, washing the clearing in warm light. Where the light touches his bruised armor seams, the bleeding stanches and torn flesh stitches itself whole in seconds.
}
"Against ordinary opponents, my steel and healing are more than enough," he continues, rolling his mended shoulders. "But that cutpurse had half a dozen enchanted blades dancing around me at once, striking from every blind spot. Blocking six sentient daggers with one sword and shield is an impossible equation, no matter your form. Plate armor has gaps at the armpits and knees. He only had to keep darting until blood loss wore me down."

"And you are just handing this tactical blueprint to a stranger?" I ask, raising an eyebrow. "Most tournament fighters would slit their own throats before advertising their fatal flaw."

Daren flashes a brief grin. "You stuck your neck out for a stranger in a death tournament. That buys you a measure of trust—not infinite trust, mind you; I am not a fool—but enough to share a road for a while."

"Pardon me for interrupting," a woman's voice calls from behind the trees. "Would you happen to be Daren, the legendary savior of the Eastern Continent?"

choice("Next chapter", Ch2-Intro, v_current_scene = Ch2-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: Ch1-Ordinary
TEXT:

"I'm not a stillwater, Daren. That thug just assumed I was and I took advantage of the situation."

"Then you cannot be a native of this land, either," Daren says, brow furrowing. "Otherwise the warden's ward would have flared the moment we crossed paths."

He means the suppression weave the tournament arbiters anchored to every participant before the teleportation gate opened. Harming Varathian civilians is strictly forbidden. The enchantment triggers an instinctive mental alarm whenever a native approaches. Accidental violence is forcibly restrained before the blow lands; deliberate attacks result in instant disqualification and banishment from the island.

The ward also doubles as a direct link for the organizers to beam individual trial objectives directly into our minds. For all their elaborate magical infrastructure, they never bothered to verify whether contenders actually possessed magic. Or more likely, they simply didn't care if a mundane mortal chose to throw himself into the meat grinder.

"So you are a registered participant," Daren says. "What reason could an ordinary human have for entering a death match against the deadliest mages in the world?"

The answer is the same prize driving every caster on this continent, though spelling it out feels like a massive gamble.

The Magium.

No living scholar can define its exact nature or composition, but everyone knows it is the primordial wellspring of all magic in existence, anchored exclusively to Varathia. Mages crave it with religious fervor. They believe direct communion will multiply their casting reserves a hundredfold and elevate them to living gods. Popular dogma insists only seasoned mages can channel it, but two decades of obscure research convinced me otherwise: the Magium's conceptual authority is absolute enough to rewrite human nature itself and awaken magic in someone born without it.

For centuries, Varathia remained utterly sealed. Entry required royal invitations, awarded to barely a handful of diplomats a year, who were escorted under blindfold and returned with zero navigational coordinates.

Then came the proclamation. A worldwide decree by the kings of Varathia announcing an open tournament for every caster across the globe, with the grand prize being direct access to the Magium.

I knew the moment the crier finished speaking that no second chance would ever come.

"Why did you enter this tournament, Barry?" Daren asks again, his dark eyes searching mine.

choice("Tell him the truth", Ch1-Truth, v_ac_ch1_honesty = 1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Truth, v_ch2_human_reason = 1)
choice("Tell him I was forced into it", Ch1-Forced, v_current_scene = Ch1-Forced, v_ch2_human_reason = 2)
choice("Tell him I'm looking for a cure for my sick sister", Ch1-Sister, v_current_scene = Ch1-Sister, v_ch2_human_reason = 3)


ID: Ch1-Truth
TEXT:

achievement("Honesty is the best policy.",v_ac_ch1_honesty)
"I'm after the same prize as everyone else, Daren," I tell him. "I want the Magium. It has been my dream since I was five years old to become a mage, and the Magium can make that happen."

"Come now, Barry. You'll need a sharper tale than that if you expect me to swallow it."

He stops, studying my face until the humor drains completely from his eyes.

"By the gods... you mean it. Barry, I admire your nerve, but surely you understand you cannot bluff your way through the world's deadliest casters?"

"I am well aware of the math. That bluff wasn't my only card. But what about you? The songs call you one of the greatest mages alive. How does a living legend end up pinned to the dirt by a cutpurse named Dave?"

"Ah. Well. That..."

He rubs the back of his neck, sheepish.

"You see, Barry, raw power does not make a man invincible. Everyone has vulnerabilities. Mine happens to be that I never quite mastered the fundamentals of the standard shield spell."

"The shield spell? The basic kinetic ward every novice learns in their first semester? How does a renowned mage fumble that?"

choice("Continue", Ch1-Weakness, v_current_scene = Ch1-Weakness)


ID: Ch1-Forced
TEXT:

"I didn't come here by choice," I tell him, adopting a weary sigh. "Believe me, I want nothing more than to walk away and never look back. But walking away isn't on the table. All I can do now is stay alive until the dust settles."

Daren frowns in thought, then his face suddenly brightens.

"Hold on, Barry. There is another way out."

"There is?"

"The tournament restrictions! The organizers swore that any deliberate attempt to harm a Varathian native triggers an instant disqualification and a teleport back across the sea. You can exploit that! Find a local civilian, throw a clumsy punch at their nose, and the overseers will yank you out of danger before your knuckles even land!"

"I... uh. I suppose that didn't cross my mind."

"Rest easy," Daren says, clapping a heavy hand on my shoulder. "I'll keep you in one piece until we reach the nearest settlement."

"Forgive my skepticism, but I just watched a rogue take you apart at the seams. How does one of the world's great mages get brought low by someone named Cutthroat Dave, anyway?"

"Ah. Well. That..."

He rubs the back of his neck, sheepish.

"You see, Barry, raw power does not make a man invincible. Everyone has vulnerabilities. Mine happens to be that I never quite mastered the fundamentals of the standard shield spell."

"The shield spell? The basic kinetic ward every novice learns in their first semester? How does a renowned mage fumble that?"

choice("Continue", Ch1-Weakness, v_current_scene = Ch1-Weakness)


ID: Ch1-Sister
TEXT:

"It's... my sister," I say, pitching my voice low and somber. "She is dying of an incurable wasting sickness, and the only known remedy grows exclusively in Varathia. I petitioned the council for months for an entry visa, but they rejected every appeal. Entering the tournament was the only way in. I just have to survive long enough to track the cure down."

Daren rests a firm, armored hand on my shoulder, his dark eyes softening.

"That is a burden no one should carry alone. I will keep you in one piece until we reach the nearest settlement with a healer's guild—someone who can confirm whether the plant you need even grows in this region. One step at a time. Fair?"

"I appreciate the sentiment, but I just watched a rogue turn your plate armor into a pincushion. How does one of the continent's legendary heroes get dismantled by a man called Cutthroat Dave, anyway?"

"Ah. Well. That..."

He rubs the back of his neck, sheepish.

"You see, Barry, raw power does not make a man invincible. Everyone has vulnerabilities. Mine happens to be that I never quite mastered the fundamentals of the standard shield spell."

"The shield spell? The basic kinetic ward every novice learns in their first semester? How does a renowned mage fumble that?"

choice("Continue", Ch1-Weakness, v_current_scene = Ch1-Weakness)


